 - **Claims.** z-design claims one issue with `./zn status <id> preparing` and gives it back with `status <id> new`. The old prep's `new raft` over `new` issues still claims them with the raft's stamp, and `./zn status <R-id> preparing` claims an existing draft raft with its unprepared members. A raft move (`status <R-id> preparing|new`) never restamps a member that is `prepared` on `origin/main`, and refuses a member another raft holds there. `ready` (and a legacy `start`) refuses a raft unless it and its members are `prepared` on `origin/main`; the claim is made by `ready`, not `start`.
 - **Rafting** (z-raft). An issue's raft is its own `raft:` line, edited only in the rafting PR: two PRs that raft one issue edit that one line, so the second cannot merge cleanly, and its sync shows the conflict. The published claim (`new raft` over prepared issues, `fold`, `recombine`, `status <R-id> preparing`) stamps the raft only, with a fresh `preparing-…` value; recombine gives its two rafts one value, and that shared stamp is what lets the carried `status <keeper> prepared` take a member whose `raft:` on `origin/main` still names the other raft. The carried move takes a member that is prepared on `origin/main` as it is, refuses one another raft holds there, and takes a `preparing` member only when it carries the raft's own stamp or a claim this checkout made (`status <id> preparing` here); any other `preparing` member is another terminal's live claim and is refused, naming it. z-design's `status <id> preparing` refuses an issue whose `raft:` names a raft on `origin/main` (z-raft takes it out first with `./zn unfold <R-id> <id>`), and `ready` refuses a raft while a member is `preparing`. A raft's `grouped:` line records a hash of each prepared member's design as z-raft grouped it (its document without `status:` and `raft:`, `check.sh`, `acceptance/`, and its design reviews, refutes and `harvest.md`); the carried move and `ready` refuse a member whose design changed on `origin/main` since (a redesign landed inside the rafting window), a member this rafting grouped with no entry, and a raft of several issues whose coherence reviews quote no `Grouped:` record covering the current one. `compact` and `renumber` re-record every entry that matched before they rewrote its ids, and `remap` does the same. `./zn release <R-id>` is the published give-back. An issue whose `raft:` names a retired or missing raft (audit's `dead-raft` error) is cleared by the command the error names for its state: in the backlog, `./zn unfold <R-id> <id>`, which claims nothing (there is no r